<!--
  ACKNOWLEDGEMENT, STYLE TWO — the same email, drawn a second way, for a side-by-side decision.

  ── IT IS A STYLE VARIANT, SO THE COPY IS HELD STILL — WITH TWO NAMED EXCEPTIONS ────────────────
  Change the words and the form at once and whichever version wins, nobody can say which change won.
  So every sentence here is the same string as `acknowledgement.mjml`, in the same order, and if one
  is edited it is edited in both files or the pair stops being a test.

  A DIFF OVER THE TWO COMPILED FILES RETURNS EXACTLY TWO DIFFERENCES, and both are forced by the
  structure rather than chosen:

    1. THE THREE FACTS ARE PACKAGED DIFFERENTLY. Style one prints them as three numbered steps;
       style two prints them as one paragraph under the stage track, because the track has already
       done the enumerating and numbering them twice would be the same list drawn twice. The words
       are the same words, and steps 01 and 02 are still verbatim `ANSWERED_BY_EMAIL` from
       `lib/submit.ts` in both files.
    2. THE PORTAL EXIT IS A LINK IN ONE AND A BUTTON IN THE OTHER, so its label differs: "See this
       case in the support portal" against "Track this case" plus "or just hit reply". That is the
       control being tested, not a copy edit, and the reasoning for keeping it optional is below.

  Anything beyond those two appearing in a diff is drift, and the check is a diff.

  ── WHAT IS ACTUALLY DIFFERENT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    1. A STATUS BAR ABOVE THE MASTHEAD. `RECEIVED` on the left, `EMAIL CASE / NO LOGIN` on the right.
       Both are permanently true of this message: it IS the receipt, and the case IS one you can work
       without an account. Neither goes stale, which is the trap the next point had to avoid.
    2. THE STAGE TRACK: `RECEIVED — IN REVIEW — ANSWERED`. This is the piece the reference was
       brought in for, and it is the piece that needed the most care. A track with the first node
       filled reads as WHERE YOUR CASE IS, and a status printed into a mailbox is a snapshot that
       goes stale — a month later this email would still be claiming the case sits at RECEIVED. So it
       is labelled `WHAT HAPPENS NEXT`, the same label style one puts over its numbered list, and it
       is the same three facts: this is the PATH, and the filled node is the stage this email is
       about, not a live position. Read that way it is permanently true.
    3. THE REFERENCE IN CORNER BRACKETS instead of a tinted callout. No fill, four 1px marks and air.
       ONE PIXEL, NOT TWO: DESIGN.md bans a coloured border over 1px on a box, and while corner
       brackets are arguably a different device from the side stripe that rule is aimed at, quietly
       reinterpreting a ban to suit a variant is how bans stop working. At 1px over a 24px arm it
       still reads as a bracket. If it should be heavier, that is a ruling to make out loud.
    4. MONOSPACED LABELS THROUGHOUT. Style one sets its micro-labels in the brand sans; this sets
       them in the mono face, which is what makes it read as an instrument rather than a letter. It
       is a real deviation from the type system, and it is the variable being tested.
    5. TWO FACTS SIDE BY SIDE instead of a ledger table. They stack under 480px on their own.
    6. A BUTTON, WHICH IS OPTIONAL AND MUST BE OMITTED RATHER THAN DISABLED. `{{ case_url }}` only
       exists for a sender who is signed in; there is no anonymous case view, so for most recipients
       there is no page behind it. Whatever renders this drops the whole button block when the
       variable is absent, exactly as it drops the `About` row. "Or just hit reply" beside it is the
       path that always works, and it is why the button can be missing without leaving a hole.

  ── THE VOCABULARY QUESTION THIS RAISES, AND IT IS NOT MINE TO CLOSE ────────────────────────────
  `RECEIVED / IN REVIEW / ANSWERED` is a SECOND status vocabulary. The product already has one for
  the customer — `Open`, `Awaiting your reply`, `Closed` — and it is on `/requests/`. These describe a
  different axis (the stages a message passes through, rather than the state a case is in) and both
  can be defended, but shipping two vocabularies to the same person is the variance this repo exists
  to fight.

  OLEH RULED ON 2026-08-24 THAT THIS IS RECONCILABLE RATHER THAN BLOCKING. It does not have to be
  settled before style two can be chosen; it has to be settled before style two is SENT. The two ways
  out are unchanged: the portal adopts these stages, or the track is redrawn in the portal's words.
  The reply's track already took the second route for its own second node, which is why it reads
  `AWAITING YOUR REPLY` and not something invented, so half the reconciliation is done.

  ── VARIABLES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Identical to `acknowledgement.mjml`; the notes are there and in the readme. `{{ space_label }}` and
  `{{ case_url }}` are the two optional ones, and the rule for both is omit the block, never blank it.

  ── THE SUBJECT, THE CLOCK AND THE LOOP HEADER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
  All three rules are unchanged and all three are argued in `acknowledgement.mjml`: the subject is
  ours and never echoes what was submitted; no text here names how long anything takes; and this is
  machine-sent, so the sending code must set `Auto-Submitted: auto-generated`.
-->
<mjml>
  <mj-head>
    <mj-include path="./partials/head.mjml" />
    <mj-preview>Your reference is {{ case_ref }}. Reply to this email to add to it.</mj-preview>
  </mj-head>

  <mj-body>
    <mj-section padding="0"><mj-column><mj-spacer height="32px" /></mj-column></mj-section>

    <mj-wrapper>

      <!-- NO BAND HERE EITHER. The dark status bar that used to sit above this carried `RECEIVED`
           and `EMAIL CASE / NO LOGIN`, and both facts survive it: the first is the filled node on the
           stage track below, the second is what "no account needed" says in the sentence under it. A
           strip that repeats two things the page already states is decoration, and removing it also
           settles what this pair is actually testing — brackets, a track, boxes and mono labels
           against plain type — rather than "one of them has a black bar".

           The logo takes the same two-file swap as the other three: it sits on the card, and the card
           is white in one theme and #111111 in the other. -->
      <mj-section border-radius="12px 12px 0 0" css-class="bo-band-top" padding="52px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-raw>
            <img src="https://support.baseout.com/email/baseout-logo-ink@2x.png" alt="Baseout" width="150" height="40" class="bo-logo-light" style="display: block; width: 150px; height: 40px; border: 0; outline: none; text-decoration: none; color: #156e97; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.01em;" />
            <!--[if !mso]><!-->
            <img src="https://support.baseout.com/email/baseout-logo-cyan@2x.png" alt="Baseout" width="150" height="40" class="bo-logo-dark" style="display: none; width: 150px; height: 40px; border: 0; outline: none; text-decoration: none; color: #5cceff; font-size: 20px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -0.01em;" />
            <!--<![endif]-->
          </mj-raw>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <mj-section padding="24px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text mj-class="h1">We have your message</mj-text>
          <mj-spacer height="12px" />
          <mj-text>Everything about this conversation lives under one reference.</mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <!-- THE REFERENCE IN BRACKETS. Three rows of a plain table: two corner marks, the value, two
           more corner marks. Borders on table cells are the one drawing primitive every mail client
           agrees on, which is why this is not four positioned spans. -->
      <mj-section padding="28px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%" padding="0" css-class="bo-bracket">
            <tr>
              <td style="width: 24px; height: 16px; border-top: 1px solid #156e97; border-left: 1px solid #156e97;">&#160;</td>
              <td>&#160;</td>
              <td style="width: 24px; height: 16px; border-top: 1px solid #156e97; border-right: 1px solid #156e97;">&#160;</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td colspan="3" align="center" style="padding: 12px 8px;">
                <div style="font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.08em; line-height: 1.4; color: #646464;" class="bo-muted">YOUR REFERENCE</div>
                <div style="font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 20px; letter-spacing: 0.02em; line-height: 1.4; color: #111111; padding: 8px 0; word-break: break-word;" class="bo-ink">{{ case_ref }}</div>
                <div style="font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; color: #646464;" class="bo-muted">Quote it in anything you send us about this.</div>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td style="width: 24px; height: 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid #156e97; border-left: 1px solid #156e97;">&#160;</td>
              <td>&#160;</td>
              <td style="width: 24px; height: 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid #156e97; border-right: 1px solid #156e97;">&#160;</td>
            </tr>
          </mj-table>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <mj-section padding="28px 32px 0">
        <mj-column><mj-divider border-style="dotted" css-class="bo-rule bo-dots" /></mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <!-- THE STAGE TRACK. The connectors are a 1px DIV with a background inside a middle-aligned
           cell, not a `border-top` on the cell itself: a border draws at the top of the box whatever
           the alignment says, so the first version hung two stubs above the dots instead of joining
           them. Measured before the fix, and it is the kind of thing that reads as "roughly right"
           in a screenshot and as broken at full size.
           THE STAGE TRACK. Labelled as the PATH, not as a position — the argument is in this file's
           header and it is the reason the label above it is the same one style one uses. The words
           are style one's three steps, and 01 and 02 are still verbatim `ANSWERED_BY_EMAIL` from
           `lib/submit.ts`, which the sentences under the track carry unchanged. -->
      <mj-section padding="20px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text mj-class="label" css-class="bo-muted">WHAT HAPPENS NEXT</mj-text>
          <mj-spacer height="12px" />
          <mj-table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%" padding="0">
            <tr>
              <td style="vertical-align: middle; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.08em; line-height: 1.4; color: #156e97; white-space: nowrap;" class="bo-node-done">&#9679;&#160;RECEIVED</td>
              <td width="22%" style="padding: 0 12px; vertical-align: middle;"><div style="height: 1px; background-color: #d5d7de; font-size: 0; line-height: 0;" class="bo-connector">&#160;</div></td>
              <td style="vertical-align: middle; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.08em; line-height: 1.4; color: #646464; white-space: nowrap;" class="bo-node-todo">&#9675;&#160;IN REVIEW</td>
              <td width="22%" style="padding: 0 12px; vertical-align: middle;"><div style="height: 1px; background-color: #d5d7de; font-size: 0; line-height: 0;" class="bo-connector">&#160;</div></td>
              <td align="right" style="vertical-align: middle; font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.08em; line-height: 1.4; color: #646464; white-space: nowrap;" class="bo-node-todo">&#9675;&#160;ANSWERED</td>
            </tr>
          </mj-table>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <mj-section padding="16px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text mj-class="muted" css-class="bo-muted">
            A person answers by replying to this email. We do not publish response times; you get an
            email when there is an update. No account needed, and your reply joins the same case.
          </mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <!-- TWO FACTS SIDE BY SIDE. MJML stacks columns under 480px on its own, which is what should
           happen here: at 375 a value cell 130px wide would break an address in three places. -->
      <mj-section padding="24px 32px 0">
        <mj-column width="50%" padding-right="8px" vertical-align="top">
          <mj-table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%" padding="0" css-class="bo-quotebox bo-round">
            <tr><td style="background-color: #f6f7fb; border: 1px solid #d5d7de; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;">
              <div style="font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.08em; line-height: 1.4; color: #646464;" class="bo-muted">ABOUT</div>
              <div style="font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; color: #111111; padding-top: 6px; word-break: break-word;" class="bo-ink">{{ space_label }}</div>
            </td></tr>
          </mj-table>
        </mj-column>
        <mj-column width="50%" padding-left="8px" vertical-align="top">
          <mj-table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%" padding="0" css-class="bo-quotebox bo-round">
            <tr><td style="background-color: #f6f7fb; border: 1px solid #d5d7de; border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 16px;">
              <div style="font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; font-size: 12px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: 0.08em; line-height: 1.4; color: #646464;" class="bo-muted">REPLIES GO TO</div>
              <div style="font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; color: #111111; padding-top: 6px; word-break: break-word;" class="bo-ink">{{ reply_to_address }}</div>
            </td></tr>
          </mj-table>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <!-- WHAT THEY WROTE. Style two keeps its labelled-box vocabulary for the two facts above, and
           takes the QUOTATION for this one, because a quotation is a different kind of thing from a
           field: the two boxes hold values we recorded, this holds words somebody wrote. Same 4px
           bar as style one, so the pair still differs in structure and not in this. -->
      <mj-section padding="24px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text mj-class="label" css-class="bo-muted">WHAT YOU SENT</mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>
      <mj-section padding="12px 32px 0">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%" padding="0" css-class="bo-round">
            <tr>
              <td width="4" style="width: 4px; background-color: #89b6ca; border-radius: 8px 0 0 8px; font-size: 0; line-height: 0;" class="bo-qbar">&#160;</td>
              <td style="background-color: #f6f7fb; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; padding: 16px 20px;" class="bo-qbody">
                <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.6; color: #111111; word-break: break-word;" class="bo-ink">{{ submitted_subject }}</div>
                <div style="font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.6; color: #646464; padding-top: 8px;" class="bo-muted">{{ submitted_body }}</div>
              </td>
            </tr>
          </mj-table>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <!-- THE BUTTON IS OPTIONAL AND MUST BE OMITTED, NOT DISABLED. `{{ case_url }}` exists only for a
           sender who is signed in; there is no anonymous case view, so for most recipients there is
           no page behind it. Whatever renders this drops the whole block when the variable is absent,
           exactly as it drops the `About` row. "Or just hit reply" beside it is the path that always
           works, which is why the block can go missing without leaving a hole where an instruction
           used to be.

           THE COLUMN IS SIZED IN PIXELS, NOT PERCENT, and that is what puts the caption beside the
           button rather than a third of the way across the card. A percentage column parks the text
           wherever the split lands; 270px is the button plus its air, so the caption starts where the
           button ends. The alternative — one table with the button hand-written into a cell — spaces
           it perfectly and throws away `mj-button`'s VML fallback, which is the only reason the
           button is a button and not coloured text in Outlook on Windows.
           BOTH COLUMNS CARRY A PERCENTAGE AND THE PAIR SUMS TO 100, which took two wrong answers to
           reach. Sizing only the first one stacks them: MJML gives the unsized sibling `per-50`, so
           the row asks for 270px plus half of itself and wraps. Sizing BOTH in pixels stacks them
           too, and that one is not obvious — 184 + 352 is exactly the 536 available, and MJML lays
           columns out as inline-blocks, so the single whitespace character between the two divs
           tips the row over its own width. A percentage pair has no such margin of error. Under
           480px MJML stacks them anyway, which is the behaviour wanted there. -->
      <mj-section padding="28px 32px 0">
        <mj-column width="42%" vertical-align="middle">
          <mj-button href="{{ case_url }}" align="left" font-family="ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace" font-size="13px" letter-spacing="0.08em">TRACK THIS CASE &#8594;</mj-button>
        </mj-column>
        <mj-column width="58%" vertical-align="middle">
          <mj-text mj-class="muted" css-class="bo-muted">or just hit reply</mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>

      <mj-section padding="28px 32px 0">
        <mj-column><mj-divider /></mj-column>
      </mj-section>
      <!-- THE SAME TWO-LINE CLOSE AS STYLE ONE, and it has to stay the same: the close is not one of
           the things this pair is testing, so a difference here would be noise in the answer. -->
      <mj-section padding="16px 32px 32px">
        <mj-column>
          <mj-text mj-class="muted" css-class="bo-muted bo-break">
            You are receiving this because you wrote to
            <a href="mailto:{{ support_address }}" class="bo-break" style="color: #156e97;">{{ support_address }}</a>.
            If the answer never arrives, check your spam folder.
          </mj-text>
        </mj-column>
      </mj-section>
    </mj-wrapper>

    <mj-section padding="0"><mj-column><mj-spacer height="32px" /></mj-column></mj-section>
  </mj-body>
</mjml>
